To extract the type of the ReactProperties member object from the class Foo, you can use TypeScript's indexed access types. Here's how you do it:

```typescript
class Foo {
  ReactProperties: {
    a: string;
  }
}

type ReactPropertiesType = Foo['ReactProperties'];
```

This creates a type alias ReactPropertiesType that is the type of the ReactProperties property in the Foo class. The resulting type is:

```typescript
{
  a: string;
}
```

If you have an instance of Foo, you can also extract the type using:

```typescript
const foo = new Foo();
type ReactPropertiesType = typeof foo['ReactProperties'];
```

Common mistakes include trying to use Foo.ReactProperties (which does not work because ReactProperties is not a static member), or using typeof Foo['ReactProperties'] (which is incorrect because Foo is a type, not a value).

