The warning 'Non-atomic operation on volatile field 'modCount'' occurs because 'volatile' only ensures visibility between threads, not atomicity. Incrementing a volatile int (modCount++) is not atomic, so in a multithreaded context, updates can be lost or inconsistent. To fix this, replace 'volatile int modCount' with 'private final AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger(0);' and update all increments to use 'modCount.incrementAndGet();' or 'modCount.getAndIncrement();'. For example:

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

// ...
private final AtomicInteger modCount = new AtomicInteger(0);

// In all methods where modCount++ was used:
modCount.incrementAndGet();

// In the DeQueueIterator:
private final int expectedModCount;
DeQueueIterator(int start, int step) {
    this.current = start;
    this.step = step;
    this.expectedModCount = modCount.get();
}
// ...
if (modCount.get() != expectedModCount) {
    throw new ConcurrentModificationException();
}

This ensures all modifications to modCount are atomic and thread-safe, addressing the warning. Note: This change only makes modCount atomic; if full thread-safety is required for the entire class, further synchronization or concurrent data structures may be needed.

