To mock a module globally in Jest using setupFilesAfterEnv, first create a setup file (e.g., jest.setup.js) and specify it in your Jest config:

// jest.config.js
module.exports = {
  setupFilesAfterEnv: ['./jest.setup.js'],
};

In your setup file, mock the module globally:

// jest.setup.js
jest.mock('./myModule', () => ({
  fetchData: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve('mocked data from setup')),
}));

This global mock will apply to all tests unless overridden.

To use a different mock in a specific test file, mock the module again in that file:

// myModule.test.js
jest.mock('./myModule', () => ({
  fetchData: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve('mocked data from test file')),
}));

const { fetchData } = require('./myModule');

test('fetchData returns test file mock', async () => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  expect(data).toBe('mocked data from test file');
});

In this example, the test file mock overrides the global mock for that file only. Jest applies the setup file first, then any mocks in the test file. Common mistakes include forgetting to specify the setup file in setupFilesAfterEnv, confusing setupFiles with setupFilesAfterEnv, or not understanding that test file mocks override global mocks only for that file.

