Yes, in Node.js, you can implement a pull-based stream using the Readable stream API in 'paused' mode. In a pull-based stream, data is only read when the consumer explicitly requests it, as opposed to push-based streams where data is pushed to the consumer as soon as it is available.

Here's an example of a pull-based Readable stream:

```js
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class PullBasedStream extends Readable {
  constructor(data, options) {
    super(options);
    this.data = data;
    this.index = 0;
  }

  _read(size) {
    if (this.index < this.data.length) {
      // Only push data when _read is called (i.e., when consumer requests it)
      this.push(this.data[this.index]);
      this.index++;
    } else {
      this.push(null); // No more data
    }
  }
}

const data = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'];
const stream = new PullBasedStream(data);

stream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  console.log('Received:', chunk.toString());
});
```

In this example, the stream only provides data when the consumer requests it (by reading from the stream or attaching a 'data' event listener). This is the essence of a pull-based stream in Node.js. The key is to implement the `_read` method, which is called by Node.js when the consumer is ready for more data. If you want to consume data manually, you can use `stream.read()` instead of listening for 'data' events.

References:
- Node.js documentation on streams: https://nodejs.org/api/stream.html#readable-streams
- The difference between push and pull streams: https://nodejs.org/api/stream.html#two-reading-modes

def replace_variables(user_input: str, variables: dict) -> str:
    """
    Replaces variable placeholders in the form <variable_name> (up to 30 characters) in the user_input string
    with their corresponding values from the variables dictionary. Only replaces if variable is found in the dict.
    Leaves unmatched or too-long variables unchanged. Handles empty input and edge cases gracefully.
    """
    import re
    # Pattern: <variable_name> where variable_name is up to 30 characters (letters, numbers, underscores)
    pattern = re.compile(r'<([A-Za-z0-9_]{1,30})>')

    def replacer(match):
        var_name = match.group(1)
        if var_name in variables and isinstance(variables[var_name], str):
            return variables[var_name]
        else:
            return match.group(0)  # leave unchanged if not in dict or not a string

    return pattern.sub(replacer, user_input)

