To manage a maximum of 5 running coroutines in PHP 8.2, you need to use an asynchronous library or extension, as native PHP does not support coroutines. Amphp is a popular choice. Here is how you can do it:

```php
require 'vendor/autoload.php';

use Amp\Loop;
use Amp\Coroutine;
use Amp\Promise;
use function Amp\call;

function job($id) {
    return call(function () use ($id) {
        echo "Job $id started\n";
        yield new Amp\Delayed(1000); // Simulate async work
        echo "Job $id finished\n";
    });
}

Loop::run(function () {
    $maxCoroutines = 5;
    $jobs = range(1, 20); // 20 jobs to run
    $running = [];

    foreach ($jobs as $id) {
        while (count($running) >= $maxCoroutines) {
            yield Promise\any($running);
            // Remove finished coroutines
            $running = array_filter($running, function ($promise) {
                return !$promise->isResolved();
            });
        }
        $running[] = job($id);
    }
    // Wait for remaining coroutines to finish
    yield Promise\all($running);
});
```

This code uses Amphp to run up to 5 coroutines at a time. It queues additional jobs and only starts a new coroutine when one finishes. Native PHP cannot do this without an extension. Common mistakes include not using an async library, not limiting the number of coroutines, or misunderstanding how to wait for coroutines to finish. Swoole can also be used, but the approach is similar: use a pool or semaphore to limit concurrency.

