To install LLVM 17 in a Nix shell, the recommended approach is to use a shell.nix or flake.nix file that specifies the exact version of LLVM you want. Here’s how you can do it using a shell.nix file:

```
{ pkgs ? import <nixpkgs> {} }:

pkgs.mkShell {
  buildInputs = [ pkgs.llvm_17 ];
}
```

If you want to use flakes (recommended for reproducibility), you can use the following flake.nix:

```
{
  description = "Nix shell with LLVM 17";
  inputs.nixpkgs.url = "github:NixOS/nixpkgs/nixpkgs-unstable";

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs }:
    let
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      pkgs = import nixpkgs { inherit system; };
    in {
      devShells.${system}.default = pkgs.mkShell {
        buildInputs = [ pkgs.llvm_17 ];
      };
    };
}
```

After creating the appropriate file, run `nix-shell` (for shell.nix) or `nix develop` (for flake.nix) in the directory. This will drop you into a shell with LLVM 17 available. Make sure your Nixpkgs channel is up to date, as older channels may not have llvm_17. If you need a specific version of Nixpkgs, you can pin it using a flake or by specifying a commit hash in your import statement.

To get the bounding box of a deeply nested SVG object using JavaScript, follow these steps:

1. Assign an id to your nested SVG element, for example: <rect id="myRect" ... />
2. In your JavaScript, select the element:

const nestedElement = document.getElementById('myRect');

3. Use the getBBox() method to get its bounding box:

const bbox = nestedElement.getBBox();
console.log(bbox.x, bbox.y, bbox.width, bbox.height);

The bounding box is the smallest rectangle that contains the entire shape, measured in the SVG's coordinate system (not the screen). Note: getBBox() only works on SVG elements, not regular HTML elements. If your SVG is deeply nested, make sure you select the correct element by its id or another selector. Avoid using getBoundingClientRect(), as it returns the position relative to the browser window, not the SVG.

To create a composable in Jetpack Compose that changes its child's content and position based on available space (similar to container queries on the web), you can use the `BoxWithConstraints` composable. This composable provides the current constraints (maxWidth, maxHeight, etc.) to its content, allowing you to adjust the UI accordingly. Here's an example:

```kotlin
@Composable
fun ResponsiveContainer(content: @Composable (Boolean) -> Unit) {
    BoxWithConstraints {
        val isCompact = maxWidth < 400.dp
        content(isCompact)
    }
}

@Composable
fun ExampleUsage() {
    ResponsiveContainer { isCompact ->
        if (isCompact) {
            Column {
                Text("Compact mode")
                Button(onClick = { /*...*/ }) { Text("Action") }
            }
        } else {
            Row(verticalAlignment = Alignment.CenterVertically) {
                Text("Expanded mode")
                Spacer(Modifier.width(16.dp))
                Button(onClick = { /*...*/ }) { Text("Action") }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, `ResponsiveContainer` uses `BoxWithConstraints` to determine the available width. If the width is less than 400dp, it displays its children in a `Column` (stacked vertically, compact mode). Otherwise, it uses a `Row` (side by side, expanded mode). This mimics the behavior of container queries on the web, where layout and content can change based on the container's size, not just the screen size.

Note: Jetpack Compose does not have a direct equivalent to CSS container queries, but `BoxWithConstraints` is the idiomatic way to achieve similar responsive behavior. Be aware of performance considerations and avoid excessive recompositions.
